From: Robertas Tumas <robertas.t@8devices.com>
Subject: [PATCH] base-files: operator account, console login and initial console password

Operator account model of the 8devices CRA/RED firmware: root is not
used directly, day-to-day access goes through "admin" plus sudo.

- "admin" user (uid/gid 1000, home /, login shell) and a "wheel" group;
  uci-default 51-sudoers writes /etc/sudoers.d/00-wheel granting wheel
  full sudo. root keeps /root. No password hashes are shipped (the RED
  tree carried fixed root/admin hashes, which a CRA baseline must not).
  Needs the "sudo" package from the packages feed.
- The console always asks for a login: the ttylogin short-cut in
  login.sh ("login -f root" unless ttylogin=1) is dropped.
  /etc/securetty lists only pts/0-3, so root may log in on pseudo
  terminals but never on the physical serial console (busybox needs
  FEATURE_SECURETTY, set in the 8devices configs; RED's defconfigs set
  a promptless symbol that defconfig dropped, so RED's "root off serial"
  never took effect).
- No console login while a console account has no password: login.sh
  runs /usr/libexec/initial-password.sh as root before the login
  prompt, through sh so that it does not depend on the file mode. While any account that can log in on the console (uid other
  than 0 with a login shell, i.e. admin) has no password, the console
  shows a password dialog for it instead of the login prompt: minimum
  length from the rpcd password policy (default 8), typed twice, set
  with busybox passwd; interrupt signals are ignored. Without this,
  "root off serial" was worth nothing: log in as admin with an empty
  password, then sudo, which accepts the empty password as well.

Ported from lab.8devices.com/red/openwrt commits "package/base-files:
add admin user", "add wheel group with sudo privileges",
"config_generate: prompt for login on console", "login: prompt for TTY
login irrespective of config" and "securetty: remove serial ttys"; the
initial password dialog is new.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Robertas Tumas <robertas.t@8devices.com>
